Cross-registration	both directions at once	Model B: 96 and 119 students going each way (2015)
Three refinements:
The name flips the old order. The merger story has Harvard leading and MIT resisting. The Broad's name puts MIT first, which is one flip. This is an observation. I found nothing saying the order was chosen for that reason.
Cross-registration is superposition. Each school is the host for the other's students, so neither is only function or only argument.
The order of the names is itself unstable. The Broad says "MIT and Harvard," while the Crimson headline writes "Harvard-MIT." Each institution may simply favor its own name first, so the flip may come from house style and not friendship.
A toy prediction: since C has period 2, a future joint institute would lead with Harvard. This is playful and not a forecast.
7. Limits of the analogy
Institutions aren't persons, and "friendship" is a metaphor here.
MIT and Harvard also compete, and the cooperation I cite doesn't capture that.
Nothing I found says the naming orders express turn-taking.
A fixed point is a mathematical object. Friendship is a lived practice.
8. Open questions
Can a typed coinductive version of the UMHF exist without breaking the stipulation?
Can alternation and superposition be unified, for example as probabilistic choice weighted toward whoever led last?
Does the model extend to a network of institutions, where turn-taking becomes a cyclic group action (Z/n)?
